# Operational persistence (Phases 33 and 34).
#
# The store writes one JSON document per committed timetable into
# `data/schedules/`, plus a `previews/` subdirectory holding the
# generation each commit is made FROM, plus a `.version-NNNNNNNNNNNN`
# hard link per version ever allocated. Those are OPERATIONAL DATA, not
# source: a committed schedule is a property of one school's
# deployment, it can be a few MB, and there is no merge semantics for a
# file whose contents depend on a local dataset. Committing them would
# put a machine's generated timetable into version control and make
# every commit noisy.
#
# The store RECREATES the directory on start, so deleting it loses
# committed schedules and previews and nothing else. If a schedule must
# survive a clean checkout, copy the directory out before cleaning.
#
# Note that deleting it also means the open tabs can no longer commit
# the generations they are showing: the preview they would commit from
# lived here, and its integrity hash cannot be re-derived once the
# bytes are gone. Phase 34 made that recovery possible across a
# restart; it cannot make it possible across a deletion.
data/

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Local env (`.env.example` IS tracked — it is the documented template)
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Editor / OS noise
.vscode/*
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Probe scripts written during ad-hoc investigation, kept out of the
# test glob by name rather than by deletion.
_probe_*.mjs
_load.mjs
